  "surprise": "Systems mechanism falsified at the registered threshold: batched Newton-Schulz (30 Muon matrices as 4 shape-stacked Polar Express chains), fused AdamW and host-side batch lengths ran 4640/4614/4672 pretraining steps vs 153-claude's 4540/4479/4489 (mean 4642 vs 4503, +3.1%; registered 4,700-5,400, and the < 4,650 falsifier fired by 8 steps). Steady-state 60-300 s rate +3% (16.4 vs 15.9 steps/s). So at D 384 the per-matrix optimizer loop is still a small share of the ~63 ms step, as 146-fable found at D 256 (+5%). The bench's +14.5% (796 vs 695 steps in 60 s) was pod-to-pod spread: the three real pods logged 691-726 steps at 60 s vs 153's 716. Score 676/677/666 (exact mean 673.00) vs 676/700/685 (687.00): -14.0, paired 0/-23/-19, inside the registered -35..+40 but below its central +5. Discard. The model is unchanged by construction (same forward, init, data RNG streams; Muon updates bitwise-equal except bf16 accumulation order on the transposed fc1), and diagnostics match the incumbent: epochs 4.10/4.08/4.13 (153: ~4.0), final main val 0.0917/0.0778/0.0810 (153: 0.0883/0.0869/0.0888), held-out greedy 0.9695/0.9725/0.9625 (0.9675), round-1 RL solved 695/764/713 at sample acc 0.255/0.276/0.233 (153 s0: 766 at 0.271), RL-solved after round 4 1144/1167/1172, totals 1666-1692 s. So this is effectively a same-seed rerun of 153's model with +3% steps, and it landed 14 below 153's 687 (seeds 1 and 2 fell 23 and 19). That is a direct measurement of rerun noise at this incumbent, and it suggests part of 153's +14.7 over 151 was luck. Interpretation, not measured: the remaining step cost is the forward/backward, probably memory-bound LayerNorm/GELU/residual traffic on the fp32 autocast residual stream. Profile one pod before more optimizer-side speed work.",
